WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.370
We are living in a special century. The
first in Earth's history when one

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:13.170
species is empowered to control the fate
of the entire biosphere. To jumpstart a

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.039
transition to entities that far
transcend our limitations. Or, to take a

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:22.140
darker view, to foreclose the immense
future potential and leave an anarchic

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:29.640
and depleted planet. So how can we
forestall catastrophes and ensure a

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.910
brighter future? We need to think
globally.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:38.879
We need to think rationally—empowered by 21st century technology but guided by

00:00:38.879 --> 00:00:43.460
values that science itself can't provide. And above all, we need to think long-term.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.870
A secular organization, The Long Now
Foundation, will create a symbol to contrast

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.160
dramatically with our currently pervasive short-termism. In a cavern deep

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.690
underground, a massive clock will be
built, designed to tick for 10,000 years,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.390
to resound with a different chime every day over that expanse of time. Those of us

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:07.290
who visit it this century will
contemplate a monument built to outlast

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:12.479
the cathedrals and will be inspired to
hope that 100 centuries from now it will

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:16.860
indeed still be ticking and that some of
our progeny will still be there to visit

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.020
it. And I give the very last word to
one of my scientific heroes, the eloquent

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:28.650
biologist Peter Medawar. I quote:
"The bells that toll for mankind are like the

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:34.830
bells of Alpine cattle. They're attached
to our own necks, and it must be our fault

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:45.459
if they don't make a cheerful and melodious sound." Thank you very much for listening.

